# Schema Comment Scope

Comments in the entity schema explain what an element **is** and why it exists —
its durable, local purpose. They must NOT reach out and describe narrow downstream
detail (a specific consumer, an exact storage path, a service name, a control-plane
section, a current algorithm). That detail lives with the code that owns it, drifts
independently, and turns the schema comment into rot the moment the consumer changes.

A schema element is used by many places over its life; a comment that pins it to one
of them is too narrow and usually wrong within a release.

```xml
<!-- GOOD: the element's own durable purpose -->
<!-- Customer: the account that owns Surveys — the ownership layer between the
     tenant (Jurisdiction) and its data. -->
<item name="Customer" ...>

<!-- BAD: reaches into a specific consumer / exact path / control-plane section -->
<!-- Customer: ... and the root of the raw-artifact storage path
     ({jurisdiction}/customers/{customer_id}/surveys/... — control-plane 4.6a). -->
<item name="Customer" ...>
```

## Rule of thumb

- Describe the element, not the pipeline it happens to feed today.
- No exact paths, service/class names, method names, or doc/section numbers.
- If it would go stale when a consumer is refactored, it belongs in that consumer.
- Prefer no comment over a narrow one; self-evident names need none.
